    <p class="qa-pillar">Myth · MYTH·15</p>
    <h1>Myth: Ravens Only Live in Cold Climates</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <div class="id-verdict verdict-false">Verdict: False</div>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens are strongly associated with cold, northern landscapes in folklore and popular imagination — but the Common Raven ranges from Arctic tundra to Saharan desert and the hot Sonoran Desert of North America, where temperatures regularly exceed 40°C. Ravens are among the most climatically adaptable birds on Earth.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Myth's Origin</h2>
    <p>The image of the raven as a cold-climate bird reflects the cultural traditions in which ravens are most prominent. Norse, Celtic, Siberian, Inuit, and sub-Arctic Indigenous traditions feature the raven heavily because ravens are indeed abundant and conspicuous in cold northern environments — following reindeer herds, feeding on the carcasses of animals killed by winter, and nesting early in the year when snow still covers the landscape.</p>
    <p>Gothic and Romantic literature further reinforced the association — Poe's raven arrives in December; ravens in folklore haunt bleak moors and foggy cliffs. The cultural weight of these northern associations has led many people to assume ravens are cold-climate specialists.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Reality: One of the Widest Ranges of Any Bird</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>) is one of the most geographically and climatically versatile birds in the world. Its range spans:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Arctic:</strong> year-round on the coasts of Alaska, Canada, Greenland, Norway, and Siberia, including areas that are ice-covered for much of the year. Ravens on Baffin Island survive temperatures of −40°C.</li>
      <li><strong>Boreal and temperate forest:</strong> across North America, Europe, and Asia — the core of the range.</li>
      <li><strong>Mediterranean and semi-arid:</strong> across southern Europe (Spain, Italy, Greece), North Africa, and the Middle East.</li>
      <li><strong>Hot desert:</strong> the Sonoran Desert of Arizona and Mexico, the Mojave Desert, the Sahara, and the arid interior of the Arabian Peninsula — environments with summer temperatures exceeding 45°C.</li>
      <li><strong>High altitude:</strong> the Himalayas above 5,000 m, the Tibetan Plateau, and the mountains of Central Asia — cold, but at tropical latitudes.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Ravens in Hot Desert Environments</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven is a resident species in the Sonoran Desert — one of the hottest deserts in North America. Behavioural thermoregulation allows ravens to function in extreme heat:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Activity is concentrated in cooler morning and late afternoon hours.</li>
      <li>Midday is spent resting in shade — cliff overhangs, under saguaro cactus ribs, in tree shade.</li>
      <li>Gular flutter (vibrating the throat pouch) promotes evaporative cooling of the blood supply to the brain.</li>
      <li>Dietary flexibility allows ravens to obtain water from food (cactus fruit, fresh prey) when water sources are limited.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Raven populations in the Sonoran Desert and Mojave have actually increased since the mid-20th century, driven by food subsidies from human development — landfills, roadkill on expanding highway networks, agricultural operations.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Ravens Actually Need</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens are not tied to any specific climate — they are generalists that require: (1) adequate food (carrion, invertebrates, plant food, human waste); (2) suitable nest sites (cliff ledges, tall trees, structures); and (3) the absence of heavy persecution. Where these conditions are met, ravens can establish themselves regardless of temperature. Their cognitive flexibility — ability to exploit novel food sources, navigate unfamiliar environments, and adjust behaviour to conditions — makes them one of the most broadly adaptable birds.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

